      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, you can measure infrasound using specialized microphones (microbarometers) that detect pressures below 20 Hz, or by attaching a sensitive accelerometer to a surface like glass to capture vibration. Given your symptoms and the HVAC context, this could likely be caused by internal mechanical motors (like fans or chillers) operating at specific frequencies that resonate with your chest cavity, though psychological stress from noise also plays a huge role. You might start by trying to find publicly available building noise maps or hiring an industrial hygienist who can perform a targeted sweep with the proper equipment at your source.",
