    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that's exactly what they'd want you to think, right? that it's too expensive or too hard to implement. i'm an architect, i know how these integrated systems work—everything is connected to a central management server. if you can control the power load of the lighting and the fan speeds of the HVAC simultaneously, you basically have a giant oscillator. if it's not for behavioral regulation, then why is it so suspiciously consistent? i'm starting to feel like i'm the only one noticing because i'm actually paying attention to the patterns. how would someone even begin to shield themselves from something that's coming from the actual structure of the walls and vents?",
